//! member: true
//!
//! std.process — build a command as an argument VECTOR, start it — alone,
//! or with a set of listeners handed down to it — wait for its exit code,
//! kill it, and end this process. Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **The PARENT half of descriptor inheritance lives here** (`start_with`,
//! sc39), and the child half is `std.net.adopt_listener` (sc37). A prefork
//! master binds its listeners once, spawns each worker with the set, and
//! each worker adopts by POSITION — descriptors `3, 4, …` in the order
//! given, `[os.proc.inherit]`. Until sc39 that handshake was written
//! across two tiers, the parent through the builtin and the child through
//! `std.net`, which is the one thing wolf-std#6's consumer named that was
//! wolf-std's to fix rather than a consumer's to accept (wolf-std#9). **The
//! set is an argument of the SPAWN and never a field of the `Command`** —
//! the reasoning is on `start_with`, and it is about lifetime: a listener
//! set belongs to the master and each spawn borrows it.
//!
//! **There is no shell here, and there never will be.** The builtin tier
//! takes an argv ARRAY and nothing else (`os_spawn(List[str])`), so a
//! command is a program name plus its arguments as separate values, all
//! the way down to the host's `execve`. No string is ever parsed into
//! words, no `sh -c` exists to parse one, and a file name containing a
//! space, a quote or a `;` is therefore just an argument. Injection is off
//! the table by construction rather than by escaping, which is the whole
//! reason this module's builder shape is the ONLY way to spawn: a
//! convenience that took `"grep -n {pattern} {path}"` would have to invent
//! quoting rules, and every quoting rule anyone has ever written has had a
//! hole in it.
//!
//! **Capability: `Exec` (I13).** `start`, `run`, `wait`, `kill` and `exit`
//! reach the host's process table, are tagged `exec` in the toolchain's
//! sandbox table, and are categorically refused at comptime (D33) — with
//! the sandbox table's own reason, which is worth quoting because it is
//! sharper than "determinism": *compiling a package must never run or
//! terminate programs on the machine that compiles it*. `wolf add` may not
//! mean arbitrary code executes with the builder's credentials. The
//! refusal is `E0701` and `tests/process/comptime_refuses.lu` holds it one
//! level down, at `os_kill`, because a `comptime fn` cannot call across a
//! module boundary at this pin (F-0051) and because `os_spawn`'s `List[str]`
//! argument is outside the comptime engine's subset — the witness has to be
//! the builtin whose only argument is an `int`, with its row HANDLED rather
//! than propagated, since `?` inside a `comptime fn` is `unsupported` and
//! masks the refusal entirely (F-0069). The builder half of this module
//! (`command`, `from_argv`, `push_arg`, `argv`, `is_success`) reaches
//! NOTHING and is comptime-safe — in an os module the pure function is the
//! surprising one, so it is spelled out here and on each of them (§14).
//! "Comptime-safe" is a statement about the sandbox and not a promise that
//! you can call it: F-0051 means no `comptime fn` can call across a module
//! boundary at this pin, so the builder is comptime-safe and
//! comptime-unreachable at the same time — exactly where `std.net`'s address
//! helpers and `std.fs`'s path helpers stand.
//!
//! **Errors are rows, never traps** (D30, §14): a program that is not
//! there, a directory that will not execute, a child that died on a
//! signal, a handle that was already reaped — every one of them is an
//! ORDINARY outcome a caller handles with `else` or propagates with `?`.
//! Nothing here traps. The vocabulary is the s40 builtin tier's, adopted
//! verbatim so std adds no translation layer:
//!
//! - `not_found` — no such program (and the same tag for an EMPTY argv,
//!   which names no program at all)
//! - `denied` — the host refused to execute it (permissions, a directory,
//!   a file with no execute bit)
//! - `signal` — the child died WITHOUT an exit code: killed, or crashed.
//!   Its own outcome and never a fake code, because every fake would
//!   collide with a code a program can really return (§12: one tag per
//!   failure mode a caller can act on)
//! - `io` — every other host failure, including a forged, foreign or
//!   already-reaped handle, coarsened to one tag because the builtin tier
//!   reports one and no caller can act on the difference (§12's rule 3)
//!
//! **An exit code is an `int`, and a handle is a TYPE.** The builtin tier
//! hands out two bare integers whose meanings are unrelated — a child
//! handle from `os_spawn`, an exit code from `os_wait` — and passing one
//! where the other belongs is the mistake this module exists to make
//! impossible: `Child` is a struct, so a code can never be waited on and a
//! handle can never be compared with 0. The code itself stays an `int`,
//! because that is what it is: the number the child returned, in
//! `0..255` on every tier-1 host, with `0` meaning success (`is_success`
//! says so once so that no caller writes the convention out).
//!
//! **A child's stdio is INHERITED, and this paragraph said the opposite
//! for four sprints.** It was headed "the stdio of a child is CLOSED at
//! this pin" and said `os_spawn` wired all three streams to the host's
//! null device, so nothing a child wrote reached the parent's streams.
//! **Measured at sc39 and false**: a self-spawned child's `print` lands
//! in the PARENT's stdout on the native lane, and on the checked lane the
//! child's diagnostics land on the parent's stderr. Both spawn builtins
//! behave this way, with an empty inherit set and with a non-empty one.
//! The claim was never checkable here, because until sc39 no test in this
//! repository ran a child at all (F-0066); `tests/process/prefork_handoff.lu`
//! is the first, and it is what caught this.
//!
//! So: a child's output and diagnostics go WHERE THIS PROGRAM'S GO. Its
//! input does not: `[os.proc.spawn]` wires a child's descriptor 0 to the
//! null device, and the sentence that stood here until s215 ("a child can
//! read this program's input") was never what the clause said. A library
//! that spawns a helper is putting that helper's chatter into its
//! caller's streams — plan for it.
//!
//! **Capture arrived at s215, and it is a map, not a mode.** wolf-lang
//! s215 added `os_pipe` and `os_spawn_fds` (`[os.proc.pipe]`,
//! `[os.proc.fds]`): a `Command` now carries a DESCRIPTOR MAP, built with
//! `set_stdin`, `set_stdout`, `set_stderr`, `set_fd` and `close_fd`, and
//! `pipe()` makes the two `std.fs` files a map hands down. A pipeline is
//! two commands and a pipe; `2>&1` is one file given to two descriptors;
//! `5<file` is `set_fd(mut c, 5, f)`. The map is DATA — a list of
//! (child descriptor, file) pairs the builders keep — so nothing here
//! parses a redirection either: a shell that reads `2>&1` from a script
//! parses it itself and calls the builders. F-0065's capture half closes
//! with this; its other items stay below.
//! Filed upstream as [wolf-lang#256](https://github.com/wolffe-lang/wolf-lang/issues/256):
//! `[os.proc]` never states the posture, and `[os.proc.inherit]`'s
//! "descriptors 0, 1 and 2 are the spawn's ordinary stdio" is the only
//! sentence in the spec that touches it — "ordinary" being precisely the
//! adjective that does not decide the question.
//!
//! **Waiting is what reaps, and the handle is consumed by it** (§14's
//! handle rule, in its second home). `wait(take ch)` ends the `Child`'s
//! life exactly as `fs.close(take f)` ends a `File`'s: a second wait is a
//! static use-after-move rather than a row on every operation for a
//! condition the compiler can see, and `tests/process/use_after_wait.lu`
//! holds the rejection as `fail(E1001)`. `kill` does NOT reap — it takes
//! `mut ch` and leaves the handle alive for the `wait` that must follow,
//! because a killed child that nobody waits for is a zombie on every unix.
//! A `Child` a caller FORGES from data (`process.Child { slot: 4242 }`)
//! answers `io`, never a trap, for the same reason a missing file does: no
//! field is private at this pin, so a forged handle is a checkable
//! condition rather than a contract violation.
//!
//! **What is deliberately absent.** Each is a reviewed contract with a
//! filing behind it rather than an approximation:
//!
//! ```text
//! output(c: Command) -> str ! {not_found, denied, signal, io}
//! stdin_text(mut c: Command, text: str)
//!     A child's whole stdout as a `str`, and text written to its stdin,
//!     as one call each. Buildable since s215 from `pipe` and the map,
//!     and left out on purpose until a consumer needs the convenience:
//!     the only correct `output` reads the pipe WHILE the child runs (a
//!     child that fills a pipe nobody drains blocks forever), which
//!     wants a task, and the first consumer (pelt) does its own
//!     plumbing.
//! env_for(mut c: Command, name: str, value: str)
//! current_dir(mut c: Command, path: str)
//!     A child's environment and working directory. `os_spawn` inherits
//!     both from the parent and takes no third argument (F-0065). A
//!     caller that must change them today changes its OWN with
//!     `std.env.set` before spawning, which is a different thing and is
//!     not documented as if it were the same.
//! try_wait(mut ch: Child) -> int ! {none, signal, io}
//!     Has it finished? `os_wait` blocks and there is no non-blocking
//!     form (F-0065), so the honest answer is unavailable — and a poll
//!     loop written over a blocking wait is a lie about its own cost.
//! wait_timeout(mut ch: Child, d: Duration)
//!     Same gap as the net tier's deadline (F-0049): no builtin arms one.
//! pid(ch: Child) -> int
//!     The `Child`'s field is the MACHINE's table index, not the host's
//!     process id, so an accessor named `pid` would name something this
//!     tier does not have. Nothing reports a real pid (F-0065).
//! signal_of(…) -> int
//!     Which signal killed it. The `signal` row carries no payload
//!     because the builtin reports none; the day it does, the tag gains
//!     a payload rather than a sibling (§12).
//! run_with(c: Command, inherit: List[net.Listener]) -> int ! {…}
//!     `start_with` + `wait`, the way `run` is `start` + `wait`. Absent
//!     because the ONLY reason to hand a listener down is that the child
//!     goes on serving after the parent returns to its loop, and a call
//!     that blocks until the child exits is the opposite of that. `run`
//!     earns its place for the commonest case; this one would have no
//!     case at all, and a convenience nobody can use is surface (sc39).
//! ```
//!
//! **Lanes, and the paragraph that used to stand here was out of date.**
//! It said the checked tier was the only lane that starts a process and
//! that the native rung refused the whole module by name ("process
//! builtins in native lowering (checked lane only at s40)"). Both were
//! true when written and neither is now: **s107 (`1b149ba`) crossed the
//! process trio natively**, and the ledger has said `native = "run"` for
//! every file in `tests/process/` since. lupin was declined by design at
//! s40 and its conformance pin now carries the whole os family, so
//! `not_found_row`, `forged_handle_io_rows`, `use_after_wait`,
//! `exit_code` and `builder` are all THREE-lane today. The stale sentence
//! survived four sprints because prose is what no gauntlet reads (sc38's
//! lesson, one module over); it is corrected here at sc39 against the
//! ledger, which is the artifact that measured.
//!
//! **`start_with` is the one function whose lanes are still uneven, and
//! every word of it is a stated posture rather than a gap.** An EMPTY
//! inherit set runs on all three lanes — `[os.proc.inherit]` makes it
//! `os_spawn` with the program named apart, served everywhere. A
//! NON-EMPTY set is refused BY NAME by two of them, in one construct
//! string they both print verbatim — *fd inheritance across
//! os_spawn_with in checked execution* — because each is a binary
//! INTERPRETING a program, so a descriptor handed to "the program's
//! child" would be handed to the interpreter's child. That is a
//! whole-program `unsupported`, not a catchable row, so the non-empty
//! witness is native-only; windows is the host that would answer the
//! catchable `unsupported` ROW instead, for a different reason again (a
//! `SOCKET` is not a small stable number a parent can name by position).
//! Three refusals, three reasons, and none of them a calendar.
//!
//! **No test and no doc example in this module starts a real program**, and
//! that is a portability statement rather than a shortcut: there is no
//! program every tier-1 host has (`/bin/sh` is not on windows, `cmd.exe`
//! is not on linux) and the directive schema has no per-platform gate — so
//! a spawn test that ran anything real would be red on some supported
//! host. What every witness here uses instead is deterministic on ALL of
//! them: a program name no host has (`not_found`), an empty argv
//! (`not_found` again), and a forged handle (`io`). The happy path — a live
//! child, its exit code, its `signal` row after a kill — is proven upstream
//! in the toolchain's own unix-gated tests, and this module's inability to
//! witness it here is filed as F-0066 rather than papered over.
//!
//! **One of F-0066's three legs came off at the sc12 pin, and the filing
//! stays open — which is worth stating precisely rather than leaving to be
//! inferred from a closed finding.** The filing named the cheapest fix as
//! an `os_exe` builtin, "which lets a test spawn ITSELF", and s90 landed
//! exactly that (wolf-lang#69: `os_exe() -> str ! {io}`, the running
//! executable's path, capability `Env` and comptime-refused beside
//! `os_cwd`). So the clause "a wolf program cannot learn its own path" is
//! RETIRED: it was true from sc11 to sc12 and it is not true now. The
//! finding is not closed, because its other two legs hold. A self-spawn
//! test would run on the CHECKED lane alone — the native rung still refuses
//! the process trio by name and lupin still declines it by design, both
//! re-measured at this pin — and a program that spawns itself must tell the
//! child from the parent, which needs an argument the child reads back and
//! therefore a second capability inside the child. std wraps `os_exe`
//! nowhere at this pin: nothing in the library needs it, and a
//! `process.exe()` whose only caller would be a test this module still
//! cannot write is surface without a use.

use std.fs
use std.net

/// A program and its arguments, as the argument VECTOR the host receives.
///
/// Element 0 is the program; the rest are its arguments, one value each,
/// with no quoting and no splitting anywhere (see the module header on why
/// there is no shell). Build one with `command` or `from_argv`, extend it
/// with `push_arg`, read it back with `argv`.
///
/// The field is a `List[str]` because that is exactly what `os_spawn`
/// takes. A `Command` reaches nothing until it is started: constructing,
/// extending and reading one is pure, and the sandbox has no objection to
/// any of it — though nothing in this module is REACHABLE from a `comptime
/// fn` at this pin, because none of them can call across a module boundary
/// (F-0051). Pure here means "no capability", not "callable at compile
/// time".
pub struct Command {
    argv: List[str],
    fds: List[int],
}

/// A started child process: this machine's handle on it.
///
/// The field is an `int` because that is what the builtin tier hands out —
/// a small non-negative index into the machine's child table, never a
/// host process id (see the module header's absent-list). Reading it is
/// legal and passing a hand-built `Child` to `wait` or `kill` is not a
/// trap but a miss (`io`), because a forged handle is a checkable
/// condition and a public struct's field cannot be private at this pin.
///
/// **Staleness is prevented by the mode system**, exactly as it is for
/// `std.fs.File` and `std.net.Socket`: `wait` takes its `Child` by `take`
/// (X1), so using one after it has been reaped is a static use-after-move
/// rather than a row on every operation.
pub struct Child {
    slot: int,
}

/// A `Command` that runs `program` with no arguments.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Pure and comptime-safe — building a command
/// starts nothing. `program` is a path or a bare name; a bare name is
/// resolved by the HOST's search rules (`PATH` on every tier-1 platform),
/// which is the host's business and not this module's, and a name it
/// cannot resolve is `start`'s `not_found` row rather than an error here.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let ls = process.command("ls")
/// let vec = process.argv(ls)
/// vec.len == 1
/// vec[0] == "ls"
/// ```
pub fn command(program: str) -> Command {
    var argv = List[str]()
    (mut argv).push(program)
    Command { argv: argv, fds: List[int]() }
}

/// A `Command` from a whole argument vector: element 0 is the program, the
/// rest are its arguments.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Pure and comptime-safe. This is the
/// constructor for an argv a program already HAS — `std.env.args()`
/// forwarded to a child, a vector read from a configuration — and it is
/// why `push_arg` is not the only way to build one.
///
/// An EMPTY vector is accepted here and misses at `start` with
/// `not_found`: a vector that names no program is data a program can hold
/// perfectly well, so refusing it at construction would move a run-time
/// outcome into a place that has no row (§2).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var argv = List[str]()
/// (mut argv).push("grep")
/// (mut argv).push("-n")
/// let c = process.from_argv(argv)
/// let vec = process.argv(c)
/// vec.len == 2
/// vec[0] == "grep"
/// ```
pub fn from_argv(argv: List[str]) -> Command {
    Command { argv: copy argv, fds: List[int]() }
}

/// Append one argument to `c`.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Pure and comptime-safe. `value` is passed to
/// the child as ONE argument whatever it contains — spaces, quotes,
/// newlines and semicolons are ordinary bytes, because nothing between
/// here and the host's `execve` parses words (the module header's whole
/// subject).
///
/// `mut c` because appending is the operation's point (§3), and the call
/// site spells it. The verb is `push_arg` rather than `arg` for two
/// reasons: `push` is std's vocabulary word for appending to a sequence
/// (§8), and `std.env.arg(i)` already means "the argument at i" — a
/// program that forwards its own argv to a child imports both modules, and
/// two functions called `arg` doing opposite things in one file is a
/// collision std declines to create (§1: one concept, one name).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var c = process.command("grep")
/// process.push_arg(mut c, "-n")
/// process.push_arg(mut c, "the wolf; rm -rf /")
/// let vec = process.argv(c)
/// vec.len == 3
/// vec[2] == "the wolf; rm -rf /"
/// ```
pub fn push_arg(mut c: Command, value: str) {
    (mut c.argv).push(value)
}

/// `c`'s argument vector: the program first, then its arguments.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Pure and comptime-safe. The list is a copy of
/// what the child would receive, so pushing to it does not extend the
/// command — `push_arg` is how a command grows.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var c = process.command("wc")
/// process.push_arg(mut c, "-l")
/// let vec = process.argv(c)
/// vec.len == 2
/// vec[0] == "wc"
/// ```
pub fn argv(c: Command) -> List[str] {
    copy c.argv
}

/// The two ends of a pipe: what is written to `write` is read from
/// `read`, in order, and `read` answers `eof` once every copy of `write`
/// is closed — this program's and every child's.
///
/// Both fields are `std.fs` files, so `fs.read`, `fs.read_chunk`,
/// `fs.write`, `fs.write_chunk` and `fs.close` serve them, and either end
/// can be handed to a child with `set_stdin`, `set_stdout`, `set_stderr`
/// or `set_fd`. Neither end reaches a child unless a map names it: both
/// are close-on-exec (`[os.proc.pipe]`).
pub struct Pipe {
    read: fs.File,
    write: fs.File,
}

/// A new pipe.
///
/// Rows: `io` when the host cannot make one (the process is out of
/// descriptors). Never traps.
///
/// **Close what you handed down.** A child given `write` holds its own
/// copy; this program's copy is still open after `start`, and a reader of
/// `read` never sees `eof` while any copy is. The pipeline idiom is: make
/// the pipe, start both stages, close this program's two ends, wait.
///
/// Capability `exec` (ruling #54: a pipe exists to wire a child, so
/// `os_pipe` is charged with the spawn family, not `io`); refused at
/// comptime (D33). Delegates to `os_pipe` (wolf-lang s215,
/// `[os.proc.pipe]`).
///
/// A prose example, because a doc example imports exactly the documented
/// module (§4) and the ends are read and written with `std.fs`:
/// `let p = process.pipe()?`, `fs.write(mut p.write, "howl")?`,
/// `fs.close(take p.write)?`, then `fs.read(mut p.read, 64)` answers
/// `"howl"` and the next read `eof`. `tests/process/pipe_round_trip.lu`
/// holds exactly that.
pub fn pipe() -> Pipe ! {io} {
    let (r, w) = os_pipe()?
    Pipe { read: fs.File { fd: r }, write: fs.File { fd: w } }
}

/// The child gets `f` as descriptor `child` — a shell's `N<file`,
/// `N>file` or `N>&M`, spelled as data: `child` is the number the CHILD
/// sees, and `f` is a file this program holds (`fs.open`'s, a pipe end,
/// or `fs.stdin()`/`fs.stdout()`/`fs.stderr()` for this program's own
/// streams).
///
/// Total: nothing is checked or opened until `start`. Naming `child`
/// again replaces the earlier entry, so the last builder call wins, as a
/// shell's last redirection does. A `child` outside `0..255` is accepted
/// here and is `start`'s `invalid` row; a closed or forged `f` is
/// `start`'s `io`. Pure and comptime-safe — the map is data.
///
/// `f` is READ, not consumed: this program still owns its copy and must
/// close it (see `pipe`). Descriptors above 2 are served by the compiler's
/// machines on linux and macOS; lupin serves 0, 1 and 2 only and refuses
/// a higher one by name.
///
/// A prose example (a `File` is `std.fs`'s, which a doc example cannot
/// import, §4): with `p = process.pipe()?`, `set_fd(mut c, 5, p.read)` on
/// `process.command("cat")` plus the argument `/dev/fd/5` is
/// `cat /dev/fd/5 5<&r`; wolf-lang's `proc_fd_lanes.rs` runs that shape.
pub fn set_fd(mut c: Command, child: int, f: fs.File) {
    place(mut c, child, f.fd)
}

/// The child's standard input is `f` — `set_fd(mut c, 0, f)`.
pub fn set_stdin(mut c: Command, f: fs.File) {
    place(mut c, 0, f.fd)
}

/// The child's standard output is `f` — `set_fd(mut c, 1, f)`. With a
/// pipe's `write` end this is how a caller captures what a child prints.
pub fn set_stdout(mut c: Command, f: fs.File) {
    place(mut c, 1, f.fd)
}

/// The child's standard error is `f` — `set_fd(mut c, 2, f)`. Given the
/// same file as `set_stdout`, this is a shell's `2>&1`.
pub fn set_stderr(mut c: Command, f: fs.File) {
    place(mut c, 2, f.fd)
}

/// Descriptor `child` is CLOSED in the child — a shell's `N<&-`. Same
/// rules as `set_fd`; lupin refuses a close by name.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var c = process.command("ls")
/// process.close_fd(mut c, 2)
/// process.argv(c).len == 1
/// ```
pub fn close_fd(mut c: Command, child: int) {
    place(mut c, child, 0 - 1)
}

/// One `(child, source)` pair into the map, replacing an earlier entry
/// for the same child: `os_spawn_fds` refuses a map that names a child
/// twice, so the builders keep it a function of the child.
fn place(mut c: Command, child: int, source: int) {
    var kept = List[int]()
    var i = 0
    while i + 1 < c.fds.len {
        if c.fds[i] != child {
            (mut kept).push(c.fds[i])
            (mut kept).push(c.fds[i + 1])
        }
        i = i + 2
    }
    (mut kept).push(child)
    (mut kept).push(source)
    c.fds = kept
}

/// Start `c` and return the handle of the child, WITHOUT waiting for it.
///
/// Rows: `not_found` when no such program exists — and for an EMPTY argv,
/// which names no program at all; `denied` when the host refuses to
/// execute it; `io` for every other host failure, and for a `File` in the
/// descriptor map that is closed or forged. Two rows belong to the map
/// alone and a command without one never raises them: `invalid` for a
/// map the builders could not make well-formed (a child descriptor
/// outside `0..255`), and `unsupported` where the host places no map —
/// windows, by name. Never traps, and a call that answers `invalid`,
/// `unsupported` or the map's `io` has started nothing.
///
/// **Without a map** the child inherits this process's environment and
/// working directory, and its standard OUTPUT and ERROR: what it writes
/// goes where this program's writes go. Its standard INPUT is the null
/// device — it reads end-of-file at once and never this program's input
/// (`[os.proc.spawn]`; this doc said "it can read this program's input"
/// until s215, which the clause never did). **With a map** (`set_stdin`,
/// `set_stdout`, `set_stderr`, `set_fd`, `close_fd`) each descriptor it
/// names is what the child gets, and the rest keep that posture — so
/// `set_stdout(mut c, p.write)` with `p = process.pipe()?` is how a caller
/// CAPTURES a child's output, F-0065's half that inheriting never was.
/// A started child MUST be waited for —
/// `wait(take ch)` is what reaps it — or it stays a zombie on every unix
/// until this process exits.
///
/// **Why the name is not `spawn`.** `spawn` is one of the language's 50
/// reserved keywords — it opens a task (`s.spawn(fn() { … })`) and a
/// supervised proc (`spawn proc`) — so `pub fn spawn` is `E0008` with no
/// raw-identifier escape, exactly as `std.fs.copy_file` is not `copy`.
/// The collision is worth more than the name: a child process and a task
/// are different things with different failure models, and a std function
/// that borrowed the keyword's word for the other one would have invited
/// the confusion the type system then has to unpick.
///
/// Capability `Exec`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `os_spawn`,
/// or to `os_spawn_fds` (wolf-lang s215, `[os.proc.fds]`) when the
/// command carries a descriptor map.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var missing = process.command("wolf-sc11-no-such-program-here")
/// var child = process.start(missing) else |_| { process.Child { slot: -1 } }
/// (process.wait(take child) else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn start(c: Command) -> Child ! {unsupported, invalid, not_found, denied, io} {
    if c.fds.len == 0 {
        return Child { slot: os_spawn(c.argv)? }
    }
    var exe = ""
    var args = List[str]()
    var named = false
    for a in c.argv {
        if named {
            (mut args).push(a)
        } else {
            exe = a
            named = true
        }
    }
    if !named { return not_found }
    Child { slot: os_spawn_fds(exe, args, c.fds)? }
}

/// Start `c` with `inherit` handed down to the child, and answer the
/// child's handle WITHOUT waiting for it — the PARENT half of descriptor
/// inheritance, whose child half is `std.net.adopt_listener`.
///
/// **The child receives the listeners as descriptors `3, 4, …` in the
/// order given**, and that numbering is the whole contract
/// (`[os.proc.inherit]`). The parent never learns an OS descriptor
/// number and the child never has to be told one out of band: "the
/// listener is 3" is true by position, so a master hands its argv a
/// COUNT and never a descriptor. Descriptors 0, 1 and 2 are the spawn's
/// ordinary stdio — INHERITED from this process, exactly as they are for
/// `start` — and nothing above `2 + inherit.len` is open in the child
/// that the runtime put there. `tests/process/prefork_handoff.lu`
/// witnesses that upper bound from inside the child: with one listener in
/// the set, descriptor 3 adopts and descriptor 4 answers `io`.
///
/// **The set is on the SPAWN and not on the `Command`, and that is the
/// one design decision in this function.** It was asked as a question at
/// sc37 and answered from real code by the consumer that needed it
/// (lobo, ws18): a master binds its listeners ONCE and keeps them for
/// its whole life, because a REPLACEMENT worker has to inherit the same
/// socket; it then spawns worker 1, worker 2, … and re-spawns worker 2
/// after a `kill -9`. The listener set is a property of the MASTER and
/// each spawn is an EVENT THAT BORROWS IT. A `Command` that accumulated
/// an inherit set would have to be rebuilt or reset per spawn, and one
/// reused across spawns would silently carry state the second spawn
/// never asked for — a `Command` that remembers descriptors is a
/// `Command` that can hand a stale one down. The call-argument shape
/// makes the borrow visible at every site and has no state to get wrong.
/// Pointing the same way: the ORDER of the set IS the contract, and an
/// ordered argument at the call is where a reader looks for that; a
/// builder field is not.
///
/// **Why `List[net.Listener]` and not `List[int]`, when `net.wait` took
/// the ints.** `wait`'s ready set mixes listeners and streams and wolf
/// has no sum type that can hold both at this pin, so `wait` had no
/// typed container to take. An inherit set has one: the only thing the
/// child can DO with a handed-down descriptor is `net.adopt_listener`,
/// so at this pin the set is listeners or it is nothing. Unwrapping to
/// the builtin's `List[int]` is this function's job rather than the
/// caller's, which is the point of a typed tier. If a future pin grows
/// an adopt for connected sockets, the container question reopens with
/// the sum type — the same language dependency `wait` is waiting on, and
/// the two should be answered together rather than one at a time.
///
/// **An EMPTY set is not a special form.** `start_with(c,
/// List[net.Listener]())` is `start(c)` with the program named apart
/// from its arguments, served on every host and every tier — including
/// the two that decline the non-empty case. A prefork master that has
/// not bound anything yet, or a caller that just wants the same spawn,
/// writes the empty list and gets an answer rather than an
/// `unsupported`.
///
/// A `Command` that carries a descriptor map is `unsupported` here: the
/// listener set and the map are two spawns' contracts, and
/// `[os.proc.fds]` keeps them apart because a net handle and a file handle
/// are different numbering spaces. Use `start` for the map.
///
/// Rows: `unsupported` where the host or the tier does not serve a
/// NON-EMPTY set, BY NAME — windows (handle inheritance exists there,
/// but a `SOCKET` is not a small stable number a parent can name by
/// position) and the CHECKED machine, which refuses it because it is the
/// `wolf` binary interpreting a program, so a descriptor handed to "the
/// program's child" would be handed to the compiler's child;
/// `not_found` when no such program exists — and for an EMPTY argv,
/// which names no program at all, raised here rather than delegated
/// because `os_spawn_with` takes the program apart and an empty vector
/// has none to give; `denied` when the host refuses to execute it; `io`
/// for a bad SET as well as for every other host failure. Never traps.
///
/// **A bad set spawns NOTHING, and the ordering is load-bearing.** A
/// forged or closed listener anywhere in `inherit`, or more than 64 of
/// them, is `io` BEFORE any child exists: the runtime maps handles to
/// descriptors first, so a caller that gets `io` from this call knows no
/// program was started and has nothing to reap.
/// `tests/process/start_with_inherit_rows.lu` witnesses exactly that,
/// with a forged listener and a program name no host has — the row is
/// `io` and not `not_found`, which is the mapping happening first.
///
/// **What the parent keeps.** Handing a listener down does not consume
/// it and does not close it: `inherit` is READ, the master's listeners
/// are still the master's after the call, and a master serving N workers
/// hands the same `Listener` to each spawn in turn. The child's copy is
/// a separate descriptor that the CHILD owns from its `adopt_listener`
/// on — `net.close_listener` there closes the inherited fd and does not
/// unlink an `AF_UNIX` path, because the path belongs to the process
/// that bound it.
///
/// A started child MUST be waited for — `wait(take ch)` is what reaps it
/// — exactly as for `start`.
///
/// Capability `Exec`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `os_spawn_with`.
///
/// A prose example, because a doc example imports exactly the documented
/// module (§4) and cannot reach `std.net` to bind a listener. A prefork
/// master binds once, then spawns each worker with the set:
/// `let srv = net.listen(net.loopback(8080))?`, build `workers` as a
/// `List[net.Listener]` holding `srv`, and
/// `let w = process.start_with(cmd, workers)?` — the worker reads
/// `net.adopt_listener(3)?` and accepts. `tests/process/start_with_empty_rows.lu`
/// and `tests/process/start_with_inherit_rows.lu` are the witnesses.
pub fn start_with(c: Command, inherit: List[net.Listener]) -> Child ! {
    unsupported,
    not_found,
    denied,
    io,
} {
    var exe = ""
    var args = List[str]()
    var named = false
    for a in c.argv {
        if named {
            (mut args).push(a)
        } else {
            exe = a
            named = true
        }
    }
    if !named { return not_found }
    if c.fds.len > 0 { return unsupported }
    var fds = List[int]()
    for l in inherit {
        (mut fds).push(l.fd)
    }
    Child { slot: os_spawn_with(exe, args, fds)? }
}

/// Wait for `ch` to finish and answer its exit code, consuming the handle.
///
/// Rows: `signal` when the child died without an exit code — killed by
/// this program or by anything else, or crashed — which is an OUTCOME with
/// no number rather than a failure to report a number; `io` when the
/// handle is forged, foreign or already reaped. Never traps.
///
/// **This blocks until the child finishes.** There is no non-blocking form
/// and no deadline at this pin (F-0065), so a caller who cannot afford to
/// wait forever has to kill the child from somewhere else — which, with
/// no way to poll, means another task.
///
/// `take ch` because waiting REAPS: the child is gone from the machine's
/// table afterwards, so a second wait is a use-after-move the compiler
/// rejects rather than an `io` row at run time (§14's handle rule).
///
/// Capability `Exec`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `os_wait`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var forged = process.Child { slot: 4242 }
/// (process.wait(take forged) else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn wait(take ch: Child) -> int ! {signal, io} {
    os_wait(ch.slot)?
}

/// Kill `ch` — end the child immediately, without reaping it.
///
/// Rows: `io` when the handle is forged, foreign, already reaped, or names
/// a child that has already exited (a child that is finished is not yours
/// to kill any more). Never traps.
///
/// **A killed child still has to be waited for.** This call does not
/// consume the handle, and the `wait` that follows answers the `signal`
/// row rather than a code — that pair is the whole "stop it" idiom, and
/// skipping the wait leaves a zombie on every unix. The kill is immediate
/// and uncatchable by the child: nothing here asks a program to shut down
/// politely, because no builtin sends a gentler signal (F-0065).
///
/// `mut ch` because killing changes what the child IS, exactly as
/// `std.fs.read` takes `mut` for advancing a position: the handle's fields
/// do not move, the thing it names does (§14).
///
/// Capability `Exec`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `os_kill`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var forged = process.Child { slot: 4242 }
/// process.kill(mut forged) else |_| { }
/// (process.wait(take forged) else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn kill(mut ch: Child) -> () ! {io} {
    os_kill(ch.slot)?
}

/// Start `c`, wait for it, and answer its exit code.
///
/// Rows: the union of `start`'s and `wait`'s — `unsupported`, `invalid`,
/// `not_found`, `denied`, `signal`, `io`, each meaning exactly what it
/// means there. Never traps.
///
/// This is the whole operation for the commonest case, where a program
/// runs another program and cares only how it ended. It blocks for as long
/// as the child runs, it reaps the child (so nothing is left behind), and
/// it cannot be interrupted at this pin. It gives you the child's output
/// only through a map: `run` blocks until the child exits, so a child
/// that fills a pipe nobody is reading blocks with it — capture with
/// `start`, read the pipe, then `wait`.
///
/// Capability `Exec`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `os_spawn`
/// and `os_wait`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let missing = process.command("wolf-sc11-no-such-program-here")
/// (process.run(missing) else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn run(c: Command) -> int ! {unsupported, invalid, not_found, denied, signal, io} {
    var ch = start(c)?
    wait(take ch)?
}

/// Whether `exit_code` means the program succeeded.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Pure and comptime-safe. Zero is success and
/// everything else is failure, on every tier-1 host; this function exists
/// so that the convention is written down once, in std, instead of as a
/// `== 0` in every caller that would then have to be re-read when someone
/// wonders whether this platform is the exception. It is not.
///
/// The `signal` row is NOT a code and never reaches here: a child that
/// died without exiting did not succeed and did not fail with a number,
/// which is exactly why it is a separate outcome (§12).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// process.is_success(0) == true
/// process.is_success(1) == false
/// process.is_success(-1) == false
/// ```
pub fn is_success(exit_code: int) -> bool {
    exit_code == 0
}

/// Replace THIS program with the one `c` names — an exec, not a spawn.
///
/// The process stays (its id, its parent, its controlling terminal) and
/// its program goes: the new one receives `c`'s whole argument vector
/// (element 0 is both the program to find and the name it is handed),
/// `env` as its ENTIRE environment — `NAME=VALUE` entries, the shape
/// `std.env.vars()` answers, so `env.vars()` passed through keeps the
/// current environment and an empty list starts from none — and `c`'s
/// descriptor map (`set_fd`, `set_stdin`, …, `close_fd`) applied to this
/// process's own descriptors. One difference from `start`: a descriptor
/// the map does not name keeps what this process has, so an exec'd
/// program reads this program's standard input (a child started with
/// `start` reads the null device). Every file this program opened and
/// did not map is closed by the exec.
///
/// A program name without a `/` is searched along the `PATH` entry of
/// `env`, or `/usr/bin:/bin` when `env` has none — the new program's
/// search path, decided from the call's own arguments, not from this
/// process's environment.
///
/// **On success this function does not return.** A call that returns has
/// failed, and its row says how: `invalid` for an empty vector, an `env`
/// entry with no `=` or an empty name, or a map `start` would refuse;
/// `unsupported` on a host with no exec (windows), by name; `io` for a map
/// naming a closed file; `not_found` when no program is found; `denied`
/// when the one found may not be run. A failed exec leaves this process as
/// it was, its descriptors included.
///
/// Capability `Exec`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `os_exec`
/// (wolf-lang s225, `[os.proc.exec]`). lupin serves descriptors 0, 1 and
/// 2 in the map and refuses any other by name, as it does for `start`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var refused = false
/// let none = process.from_argv(List[str]())
/// process.exec(none, List[str]()) else |_| { refused = true }
/// refused == true
/// ```
pub fn exec(c: Command, env: List[str]) -> () ! {unsupported, invalid, not_found, denied, io} {
    var exe = ""
    if c.argv.len > 0 {
        exe = c.argv[0]
    }
    os_exec(exe, c.argv, env, c.fds)?
}

/// End THIS process immediately with `code`.
///
/// Never returns — no `defer` runs, no buffer is flushed by std, and no
/// row can be raised by a call that does not come back. Not a trap: the
/// process ends with the status the caller asked for, which is the one
/// thing a trap cannot do.
///
/// Only the low byte of `code` survives on a unix host: the exit status a
/// parent can observe is `code` modulo 256, so `exit(300)` is seen as 44
/// and `exit(-1)` as 255. std does not renumber or clamp — a caller that
/// wants a portable status writes one in `0..255`, and this sentence is
/// where that is documented rather than discovered.
///
/// Capability `Exec`; refused at comptime (D33) — a comptime program that
/// could end the compiler is the sharpest case of that rule. Delegates to
/// `os_exit`, which is the one member of the s40 `exec` family every rung
/// executes: this function therefore runs under the interpreter too, and the
/// only reason it is dark on the native rung is that lowering refuses the
/// whole MODULE for the process trio beside it.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// process.is_success(0) == true
/// process.exit(0)
/// ```
pub fn exit(code: int) {
    os_exit(code)
}
